#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151957 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "edfa7501-4de4-5087-80fe-af2a91e53487" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Greatest Common Divisor of Larger Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "2acbb9f2-ae84-536f-86aa-a1ca9203362f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6517" :question/problem "Find the greatest common divisor of $1\\,036$ and $555$." :question/worked-solution "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1036 &= \\begin{vmatrix}555(1) & +\\end{vmatrix}481 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 555 &= \\end{vmatrix}481(1)\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 74 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 481|= & 74(6)\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}37 \\\\ ↙\\end{vmatrix}↙ \\\\\n74 &= \\begin{vmatrix}37(2) & +\\end{vmatrix}0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}37}$. Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $1\\,036$ and $555$ is $37$. Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(1\\,036, 555) = 37\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151993 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9625025a-4737-54ac-b218-56909013019a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Greatest Common Divisor of Two Three-Digit Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "012fbcd1-3203-5247-8294-32bf795aadaf" :question/math-academy-id "e-6858" :question/problem "Find the greatest common divisor of $713$ and $322$." :question/worked-solution "We use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n713 &= \\begin{vmatrix}322(2) & +\\end{vmatrix}69 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 322 &= \\end{vmatrix}69(4)\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 46 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 69|= & 46(1)\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}23 \\\\ ↙\\end{vmatrix}↙ \\\\\n46 &= \\begin{vmatrix}23(2) & +\\end{vmatrix}0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{red}23}$. Therefore, we conclude that the greatest common divisor of $713$ and $322$ is $23$. Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(713, 322) = 23\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152033 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2cb28a0-b456-55cc-94c3-2a35edde21be" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Integers are Coprime"}] :question/id #uuid "d6699fbf-a942-5828-ab49-8e961e47d3c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8767" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 7$\n2. $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 1$\n3. $651$ and $574$ are coprime" :question/worked-solution "Recall that two integers are coprime if their greatest common divisor is $1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Indeed, let's use the Euclidean algorithm, as follows:\n$651|=\\begin{vmatrix}574 \\cdot 1 & +\\end{vmatrix}77; ↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 574|=\\end{vmatrix}77 \\cdot 7\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 35 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 77|= & 35 \\cdot 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ ↙\\end{vmatrix}↙; 35|=\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\cdot 5 & +\\end{vmatrix}0$\nThe last positive remainder is ${\\color{blue}7}$. So, the greatest common divisor of $651$ and $574$ is $7$:\n$\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) = 7$.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{gcd}(651, 574) > 1$, the numbers are not coprime.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9625025a-4737-54ac-b218-56909013019a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Greatest Common Divisor of Two Three-Digit Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b05a8316-08dd-5db5-8322-9cb9887200ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151092} {:answer/id #uuid "013e21d7-10d7-508a-858f-e4687a15b617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151093} {:answer/id #uuid "2e3c93cf-e65f-5d32-8cc1-fe7445050e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151094} {:answer/id #uuid "51ac2676-94e1-52d3-a7c2-0bef8812a67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151095} {:answer/id #uuid "681042cb-b356-52a1-a7cd-dc87fe9884f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151096}] :answer-field/id #uuid "de00a6fe-ee44-5900-a799-30160212f7fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151091}] :question/id #uuid "3b810733-c1e7-54dd-bed6-851e7f2c57d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-77116" :question/problem "Find the greatest common divisor of $296$ and $185$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "edfa7501-4de4-5087-80fe-af2a91e53487" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Greatest Common Divisor of Larger Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e187515-112c-5fef-8d99-85b05dad354d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151338} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c5b25f-2aca-5694-a936-b46b473d1f82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151339} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb60cdb-2cff-5660-9b07-7746ca5105af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151340} {:answer/id #uuid "f8641e55-a99c-5e75-bfc4-533debe3da92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151341} {:answer/id #uuid "219c